Step-by-step fetch:

Query q = 1 → bounds OK → output freq[1] = 2.

Query q = 4 → bounds OK → output freq[4] = 0.

Query q = 2 → bounds OK → output freq[2] = 1.

Query q = 3 → bounds OK → output freq[3] = 2.

Query q = 12 → bounds OK → output freq[12] = 0.

All fetches are constant time O(1).

4) Complexity recap

Precompute: O(N) (one pass through the array).

Each query: O(1).

Total for Q queries: O(N + Q).

Space: O(maxVal) (array of length maxVal + 1).

5) Memory / caveat for large maxVal

This method is simple and extremely fast when maxVal is small. But if maxVal is large (e.g., 10^9) it becomes impractical because of memory.

Memory calculation (int = 4 bytes):

If maxVal = 12 → length = 13 → bytes = 13 * 4 = 52 bytes (tiny).

If maxVal = 10^9 → length = 1,000,000,001 → bytes = 1,000,000,001 * 4 = 4,000,000,004 bytes ≈ 4.000 GB (decimal) ≈ 3.725 GiB (binary GiB).
(That is just the raw int data; actual Java int[] has object header and alignment overhead, so real memory is somewhat higher.)

Because typical program memory (and contiguous array allocation) and JVM overhead make such very large arrays impractical, do not use int[] freq when maxVal is huge or unknown. Instead, use a HashMap<Integer,Integer> to store only the keys that actually appear (sparse representation).

6) Safe Java code (with bounds check)
int maxVal = 12;
int[] freq = new int[maxVal + 1];
for (int v : arr) {
    if (v >= 0 && v <= maxVal) freq[v]++;
    // else ignore or handle out-of-range values as needed
}

// answer queries:
for (int i = 0; i < Q; i++) {
    int q = sc.nextInt();
    if (q >= 0 && q <= maxVal) System.out.println(freq[q]);
    else System.out.println(0);
}

7) Bottom line (when to use)

Use array hashing (int[] freq) when:

Values are non-negative, dense, and maxVal is reasonably small (fits comfortably in memory).

You need the fastest possible O(1) queries and low overhead.

Use HashMap when:

maxVal is unknown, very large, or keys are sparse (only few unique values).

You need to handle arbitrary ints without huge memory consumption.

